WEBVTT

00:00.100 --> 00:01.840
Welcome to Wonder Cabinet.

00:04.100 --> 00:05.600
I'm Anne Strangehamps.

00:05.900 --> 00:06.860
And I'm Steve Paulson.

00:08.100 --> 00:10.960
Where do you go when you need a dose of wonder?

00:11.360 --> 00:15.080
A way to lift your spirits, let go of the day's heaviness.

00:15.500 --> 00:18.220
Well, the biggest frame of reference we have is the cosmos,

00:18.800 --> 00:23.480
an entire universe of expanded horizons and limitless possibility.

00:24.040 --> 00:27.840
Where time flows differently, space bends and curves,

00:27.840 --> 00:31.320
and reality is so much more than what our senses tell us.

00:32.020 --> 00:34.160
Imagine you're a theoretical physicist.

00:34.660 --> 00:38.060
You spend your whole life on the edge of all that wonder.

00:38.580 --> 00:42.460
Every day you wake up and you do your best to understand it just a little bit better.

00:43.080 --> 00:45.500
Maybe even discover something new.

00:46.180 --> 00:50.800
What was it like for you to see that first photograph of the black hole?

00:50.880 --> 00:51.340
Do you remember?

00:51.600 --> 00:53.820
Yes, it was an incredible emotion.

00:53.820 --> 00:58.600
It's like, you know, we've been studying an animal, mysterious all your life,

00:58.660 --> 00:59.360
and then you see it.

00:59.400 --> 01:00.640
Well, it exists, it's real.

01:01.200 --> 01:06.020
This is Carlo Rovelli, and he is one of the world's most celebrated physicists.

01:06.440 --> 01:09.080
Author of a string of best-selling books, including

01:09.080 --> 01:14.520
Seven Brief Lessons on Physics, Reality is Not What It Seems, and The Order of Time.

01:14.860 --> 01:18.040
We thought we would start this hour by playing this little story

01:18.040 --> 01:22.820
that he told me a few years ago about one of his own favorite moments of wonder.

01:23.820 --> 01:28.360
The best experience has not been the picture of the black hole,

01:28.960 --> 01:30.640
which is a few years ago,

01:31.460 --> 01:33.200
but a little bit before that,

01:33.300 --> 01:36.580
there was the announce of the detection of the gravitational waves

01:36.580 --> 01:39.260
that was produced by a black hole.

01:39.260 --> 01:44.620
Since there are so many black holes in the sky,

01:45.260 --> 01:47.060
they can meet and fall into one another.

01:47.920 --> 01:49.200
They fall into one another,

01:49.580 --> 01:51.980
they start orbiting, turning around,

01:52.560 --> 01:55.320
then orbiting faster and faster, getting closer and closer,

01:55.700 --> 01:57.980
faster, faster, faster, faster, faster, closer, closer, closer,

01:58.100 --> 01:58.520
then whoop!

02:00.020 --> 02:02.240
They just eat each other, so to say.

02:02.240 --> 02:04.760
And if they do so,

02:04.940 --> 02:10.580
they produce this enormously strong oscillation of space,

02:11.160 --> 02:12.820
which is what was detected.

02:14.040 --> 02:16.640
The announcement of that was given publicly,

02:17.760 --> 02:21.020
and the press conference was during a class

02:21.020 --> 02:25.240
I was giving to my students in Marseille on general relativity.

02:26.320 --> 02:28.480
So I told the students,

02:28.780 --> 02:30.320
look, there is this press conference,

02:30.320 --> 02:31.740
they say they have gravitational waves,

02:31.800 --> 02:34.300
they have not seen what they have seen,

02:34.400 --> 02:35.280
they say they have seen,

02:35.700 --> 02:37.660
and I turned the computer on,

02:38.320 --> 02:39.340
in front of the class,

02:40.080 --> 02:42.560
but before, I gave them the theory.

02:43.680 --> 02:46.420
I explained to them that the black hole can merge,

02:46.480 --> 02:47.540
and they produce this wave,

02:48.380 --> 02:51.760
and oscillations, which first is slow and weak,

02:51.820 --> 02:53.620
and then become faster and higher.

02:54.260 --> 02:55.520
So if it was a sound,

02:55.520 --> 02:56.700
it would be something like,

02:57.020 --> 02:57.820
whoop!

02:57.820 --> 02:59.460
It's a chirp,

02:59.660 --> 03:00.900
the chirp of the black hole.

03:01.200 --> 03:01.460
Whip!

03:02.360 --> 03:05.400
And I drew the shape of this wave of the black hole.

03:06.320 --> 03:07.960
Then we turned the computer on,

03:08.400 --> 03:09.620
to the press conference.

03:10.160 --> 03:12.620
The black holes are getting closer and closer to one another.

03:12.840 --> 03:14.020
There is great emotion.

03:14.580 --> 03:15.800
They're going to merge.

03:16.420 --> 03:16.680
Boom.

03:17.260 --> 03:20.460
The first time that this has ever been seen.

03:21.000 --> 03:21.660
As I said,

03:21.660 --> 03:24.360
We can hear gravitational waves.

03:24.460 --> 03:25.960
We can hear the universe.

03:26.440 --> 03:28.460
And this is a signal that we have measured.

03:29.020 --> 03:30.540
There's the rumbling noise,

03:30.760 --> 03:32.080
and then there's the chirp.

03:32.300 --> 03:34.980
The shape of the wave appears,

03:35.460 --> 03:38.040
and is exactly equal to the shape of drone of the black hole.

03:41.040 --> 03:41.760
Whoop!

03:42.300 --> 03:44.580
That's the chirp we've been looking for.

03:44.580 --> 03:46.340
And the students,

03:46.460 --> 03:48.360
it was a class of 20, 30 students,

03:48.740 --> 03:49.420
all together,

03:49.760 --> 03:50.340
Wow!

03:50.700 --> 03:50.900
What?

03:53.620 --> 03:55.440
There's like science at it best.

03:55.620 --> 03:56.260
When you're really,

03:56.580 --> 03:57.660
when you're really science,

03:57.780 --> 03:58.980
it's shining.

03:59.800 --> 04:00.880
Gravitational waves.

04:01.140 --> 04:01.860
We did it.

04:01.860 --> 04:07.180
That is an episode we did on

04:07.180 --> 04:08.780
To the Best of Our Knowledge a while back,

04:08.840 --> 04:10.380
and today we are excited to bring you

04:10.380 --> 04:13.100
a brand new conversation with Carlo Rovelli.

04:13.460 --> 04:16.560
Recorded at the Island of Knowledge in Italy.

04:16.780 --> 04:17.680
Which, to be clear,

04:17.740 --> 04:19.280
is not an actual island.

04:19.500 --> 04:21.320
It's a small think tank in Tuscany

04:21.320 --> 04:22.500
where three times a year,

04:22.880 --> 04:24.860
small groups of scientists and philosophers

04:24.860 --> 04:28.040
come together to explore some really big questions.

04:28.040 --> 04:28.900
And this time,

04:29.160 --> 04:29.980
Rovelli was there.

04:30.280 --> 04:31.280
What do you want to talk about?

04:31.280 --> 04:32.120
What's the topic?

04:32.480 --> 04:32.760
You.

04:33.300 --> 04:33.660
Oh boy.

04:35.480 --> 04:36.040
Okay.

04:36.700 --> 04:38.000
So I want to take you back

04:38.000 --> 04:40.660
to your early years as a scientist.

04:41.220 --> 04:43.420
Why did you want to become a physicist?

04:45.920 --> 04:48.460
That was not an early call.

04:48.960 --> 04:51.020
My actual plan after high school

04:51.020 --> 04:54.040
was to go traveling the world.

04:54.640 --> 04:57.540
My ambition was to become a beggar.

04:58.680 --> 04:59.240
Really?

04:59.560 --> 04:59.760
Yeah.

05:00.100 --> 05:01.140
Travel around the world,

05:01.140 --> 05:01.760
I don't know.

05:01.760 --> 05:02.440
As a beggar.

05:02.560 --> 05:03.880
The modern St. Francis,

05:03.980 --> 05:04.880
holding out your hat.

05:04.880 --> 05:05.480
Yeah, exactly.

05:05.580 --> 05:06.100
You got it right.

05:06.220 --> 05:08.140
It was not a lack of estimate in myself.

05:08.300 --> 05:09.380
It was an overestimation.

05:09.720 --> 05:12.340
The models were St. Francis or Buddha, right?

05:12.500 --> 05:14.660
So it was an overambition.

05:14.900 --> 05:16.220
So I have read that

05:16.220 --> 05:17.760
one of your form of experiences,

05:17.840 --> 05:18.800
probably more than one,

05:18.980 --> 05:20.240
is psychedelics.

05:20.380 --> 05:21.300
When you were a teenager,

05:21.640 --> 05:22.780
LSD in particular?

05:22.780 --> 05:25.180
Well, psychedelics is even before that.

05:25.180 --> 05:25.540
Okay.

05:25.840 --> 05:27.040
In fact, I was a teenager.

05:27.120 --> 05:27.760
I was 16.

05:28.120 --> 05:28.340
Ah.

05:28.700 --> 05:32.560
I got into that because the little I read about it

05:32.560 --> 05:34.340
sounded completely fascinating,

05:34.580 --> 05:36.720
the idea of exploring state of consciousness,

05:37.400 --> 05:38.940
seeing the world differently,

05:39.120 --> 05:40.680
going with the mind, who knows where.

05:41.400 --> 05:43.920
These were the early 70s.

05:44.700 --> 05:45.820
I grew up in Verona.

05:45.820 --> 05:49.440
It was a sort of provincial small town in Italy.

05:49.780 --> 05:51.280
But I was visiting Paris.

05:52.220 --> 05:54.420
And then a friend of a friend, you know,

05:54.440 --> 05:56.320
said, oh, maybe I can get some LSD.

05:56.500 --> 05:57.060
There is somebody,

05:57.200 --> 05:59.000
a people called Los Pagnole, the Spanish.

05:59.280 --> 06:00.260
I'll ask for him.

06:01.360 --> 06:03.860
So he sell us these little pieces of paper

06:03.860 --> 06:06.260
and we have no idea whether it's just cheating us.

06:06.620 --> 06:08.520
Oh, these were like the acid blotters.

06:08.760 --> 06:09.300
Yeah, exactly.

06:10.020 --> 06:10.760
That was a first.

06:11.040 --> 06:12.940
And it was a spectacular experience.

06:12.940 --> 06:14.500
It was extremely powerful,

06:14.740 --> 06:16.900
which left a big impact on me.

06:17.240 --> 06:19.100
Why did it make such an impact on you?

06:19.960 --> 06:25.140
It upset my sense of what I knew about reality entirely.

06:25.940 --> 06:26.800
I was very young.

06:26.880 --> 06:28.540
I mean, this was before my first girlfriend.

06:29.100 --> 06:29.320
Yeah.

06:29.480 --> 06:30.600
I mean, 16.

06:31.380 --> 06:31.640
16.

06:31.840 --> 06:34.400
That's young to start taking psychedelics.

06:34.720 --> 06:35.620
LSD in particular.

06:35.620 --> 06:36.660
It is young, yes.

06:37.100 --> 06:39.120
I had the good fortune of a friend

06:39.120 --> 06:43.900
that gave me the simplest, best instruction about it.

06:44.420 --> 06:46.940
She said, look, whatever happens, don't worry, don't panic,

06:47.140 --> 06:48.240
don't resist, let it go.

06:48.360 --> 06:49.280
You're going to come back.

06:49.640 --> 06:53.920
So, I mean, there was a first phase in which just dancing colors

06:53.920 --> 06:58.740
and, you know, the houses becoming fat and beautiful.

06:59.480 --> 07:00.340
All this.

07:00.580 --> 07:00.820
Right.

07:00.920 --> 07:02.880
What were solid lines became wavy.

07:03.400 --> 07:05.280
Yeah, everything wavy and all this theater.

07:05.640 --> 07:07.600
And we were, the three of us, super happy.

07:07.600 --> 07:11.200
And then, but that was only the beginning.

07:11.300 --> 07:14.640
Then at some point, I felt this wave arriving on me

07:14.640 --> 07:16.560
and I just sat down under a tree.

07:16.720 --> 07:18.020
We were in a park in the night

07:18.020 --> 07:22.020
and put my hands in front of my eyes

07:22.020 --> 07:25.060
and just felt that I was going totally elsewhere.

07:25.220 --> 07:30.600
I remember the first images were this huge red color

07:31.160 --> 07:34.700
that was opening and me going in and then closing behind me

07:34.700 --> 07:37.980
and then this blue screen and opening and me going.

07:38.280 --> 07:40.440
And I said, oh boy, what's going on here?

07:40.980 --> 07:44.740
There was no, nothing of what was familiar around.

07:45.000 --> 07:46.560
No space, no time, no myself.

07:47.060 --> 07:49.360
With incredible beauty, incredible emotion,

07:49.620 --> 07:51.060
incredible intensity.

07:51.260 --> 07:53.780
Was it a single experience or did you have a few more like that?

07:53.780 --> 07:54.080
No, I repeated it.

07:54.260 --> 07:55.500
I'm talking about the first one

07:55.500 --> 07:58.760
because it was really the one which opened up a world for me.

07:58.760 --> 08:01.900
I repeated it not many times,

08:01.900 --> 08:06.400
maybe in my life less than 10 times during my youth.

08:06.980 --> 08:07.760
Then I stopped.

08:10.220 --> 08:12.500
What did I took away from that?

08:14.100 --> 08:15.840
You know, I do quantum gravity.

08:16.040 --> 08:17.260
I do fundamental physics.

08:17.440 --> 08:18.060
What is space?

08:18.160 --> 08:18.760
What is time?

08:19.440 --> 08:20.500
This seems like good training.

08:20.740 --> 08:21.360
Yeah, exactly.

08:21.360 --> 08:22.960
To become a theoretical physicist,

08:23.280 --> 08:25.140
to question the nature of reality.

08:25.380 --> 08:26.280
In a sense, yes.

08:26.280 --> 08:32.420
I think there was a liberating, intellectually liberating thing.

08:32.740 --> 08:38.700
The sense that reality can be profoundly different from our everyday.

08:39.080 --> 08:44.220
The everyday view of reality, probably rooted in that,

08:44.420 --> 08:50.440
I've always felt is like our sleepwalking without seeing what's behind it.

08:50.720 --> 08:53.740
I mean, you're talking about experiences you had 50 years ago.

08:53.740 --> 08:55.060
I'm talking about, yeah, yeah, yeah.

08:55.080 --> 08:56.600
And you're saying you still think about those.

08:56.800 --> 08:57.680
I still think about that.

08:58.000 --> 08:58.200
Yeah.

08:58.520 --> 09:02.240
So you went on, of course, to become a major theoretical physicist.

09:02.440 --> 09:03.060
Yeah, much later.

09:03.300 --> 09:04.600
I went to physics 10 years later.

09:04.880 --> 09:09.100
But it sounds like those experiences maybe help push you in the direction

09:09.100 --> 09:10.560
of wanting to become a physicist.

09:11.040 --> 09:11.700
Yeah, maybe.

09:11.860 --> 09:12.740
I was, yeah, sure.

09:12.800 --> 09:14.100
I was rebellious as a kid.

09:14.100 --> 09:21.080
I grew up in a loving family and in a provincial Italy, a bit fascist.

09:21.700 --> 09:23.720
I was covered by love by my mother.

09:23.940 --> 09:26.920
You know, love is the greatest thing around, but sometimes it's heavy.

09:27.520 --> 09:28.940
I needed to run away.

09:30.480 --> 09:37.160
The same summer in Paris, I met a friend from Sofia, Sofia, Bulgaria,

09:37.560 --> 09:40.000
which at the time was the other side of the Iron Curtain.

09:40.120 --> 09:40.940
It was the Soviet world.

09:40.940 --> 09:44.640
We went into enormous political discussions and he kept saying,

09:44.740 --> 09:45.740
yeah, you should come and see.

09:46.620 --> 09:47.480
And so I decided to go.

09:48.120 --> 09:52.240
So 16 alone, I hitchhiked through Europe from Paris to Sofia,

09:52.360 --> 09:55.220
a thousand kilometers, sleeping in the fields.

09:55.680 --> 09:58.440
I crossed into Soviet world.

09:59.140 --> 10:02.780
And that was another liberating, mind liberating experience for me.

10:02.920 --> 10:07.620
I realized that the Soviet world was nothing like we were told

10:07.620 --> 10:08.900
by the Western propaganda.

10:08.900 --> 10:14.580
But the shocking thing was that the Soviet propaganda was describing

10:14.580 --> 10:17.680
a Western world, which is nothing like my experience.

10:18.120 --> 10:22.260
So I realized that Europe was living at the time split in two parts.

10:22.400 --> 10:26.460
Each one was telling a story about the other, which is completely fake.

10:26.820 --> 10:31.540
And since then, I became very sensitive to propaganda somehow.

10:31.880 --> 10:32.720
I listened to the news.

10:32.720 --> 10:37.580
And even today, I spend my time listening to news from all over the world.

10:37.780 --> 10:39.260
I regularly make the tour.

10:39.400 --> 10:42.580
I listen to the Chinese, to the Indians, to the Brazilian, to the Iranians,

10:42.700 --> 10:46.080
to the South Africans, to the Americans, the British, the Italian.

10:46.560 --> 10:49.900
And it's so obvious that everybody's telling stories.

10:50.400 --> 10:53.360
Well, and it's just, I mean, going back to those early years, too.

10:53.440 --> 10:55.800
I mean, you said you started getting really interested in politics.

10:55.800 --> 10:58.340
I mean, I know you became a student radical.

10:58.640 --> 11:01.180
I mean, literally, manning the barricades.

11:01.560 --> 11:03.360
I was a student radical.

11:03.660 --> 11:08.780
There was a rebellion movement in Italy in the late 70s,

11:08.800 --> 11:11.780
which was a long way from the sort of the 60s.

11:12.500 --> 11:15.060
So, yes, I was involved in this radical politics.

11:15.200 --> 11:17.800
I was fascinated by radical movements.

11:17.800 --> 11:22.240
I mean, from Che Guevara to Mao Zedong, there was that generation.

11:22.600 --> 11:26.260
And you were arrested at one point, right, for refusing military service?

11:26.340 --> 11:32.880
I was arrested for refusing military service because I was strongly pacifist in that sense.

11:33.660 --> 11:36.180
I don't believe in borders, even today.

11:36.520 --> 11:38.480
I don't believe in patria.

11:38.780 --> 11:40.360
They say I'm not patriotic.

11:41.420 --> 11:41.940
Patriotism.

11:42.060 --> 11:43.340
I don't believe in patriotism.

11:43.340 --> 11:47.060
I believe in the fact that we are a single tribe all over the planet.

11:47.820 --> 11:49.340
So, I want to come back to this.

11:49.420 --> 11:50.580
I want to come back to politics.

11:50.800 --> 11:55.040
But I want to spend a little bit of time talking about your work as a physicist.

11:55.780 --> 12:03.740
Well, that was somehow the moment of political engagement ended up with quite a disappointment.

12:04.580 --> 12:07.920
To put it brutally, we want to change the world,

12:08.020 --> 12:12.140
made a revolution for a more gentle world without weapons, without borders.

12:12.140 --> 12:12.820
And we failed.

12:12.980 --> 12:13.780
We lost the revolution.

12:14.420 --> 12:16.180
So, I had to find something else to do.

12:16.180 --> 12:22.280
And at that precise moment, I fell in love with physics.

12:22.740 --> 12:25.480
It was madly falling in love with physics.

12:26.180 --> 12:27.640
Why did you fall in love with physics?

12:27.640 --> 12:32.100
Because quantum mechanics and general relativity are the most beautiful human creations.

12:33.280 --> 12:34.600
First, Einstein theory.

12:34.700 --> 12:36.740
General relativity is incredibly spectacular.

12:37.220 --> 12:38.540
It's better than LSD.

12:38.540 --> 12:45.160
I mean, it's telling that reality is profoundly different than what we instinctively think.

12:45.160 --> 12:47.200
But in a way, that works so well.

12:47.280 --> 12:50.060
I mean, there's this curving of space, stretching on time.

12:50.720 --> 12:55.180
And yet, this works fantastically to describe things which are really on the universe.

12:55.420 --> 12:57.920
Gravitation waves, black holes, suspenseful universe.

12:58.460 --> 13:03.020
So, it was the sense that you were getting closer to sort of what the nature of reality was?

13:03.120 --> 13:04.680
Was that the fascination?

13:04.800 --> 13:07.120
Or was it sort of at a mathematical level?

13:07.300 --> 13:08.260
No, it was not mathematics.

13:08.260 --> 13:10.200
I would have never been fascinated by mathematics.

13:10.300 --> 13:11.280
I'm bored by mathematics.

13:11.560 --> 13:13.060
I'm not a good mathematician.

13:13.360 --> 13:14.640
I have to struggle through mathematics.

13:15.120 --> 13:19.420
But it's sort of, my sense is, it's sort of like, I mean, the great thing about theoretical

13:19.420 --> 13:24.120
physics is, I mean, you're sort of cracking the code in a sense of, you know, how all of

13:24.120 --> 13:27.000
this, you know, is structured, came into being in a way, right?

13:27.280 --> 13:27.980
Yes and no.

13:28.100 --> 13:34.200
I would say it's a strong feeling of raising a veil and seeing something that you couldn't

13:34.200 --> 13:34.800
see before.

13:35.740 --> 13:37.740
Understanding profoundly better reality.

13:37.740 --> 13:40.880
But that doesn't mean cracking the code.

13:41.500 --> 13:47.420
And I never thought, and I still don't think, that science is getting at the bottom of things.

13:47.520 --> 13:48.800
It's getting one more layer.

13:49.160 --> 13:50.820
But maybe there's another layer.

13:50.960 --> 13:52.200
There's another layer again.

13:52.520 --> 13:55.920
I don't like the idea that we are getting to the bottom.

13:56.500 --> 14:00.180
Well, I'm surprised that you say that because, I mean, your work on quantum gravity, I mean,

14:00.220 --> 14:04.320
one of the things that you're famous for is this effort to reconcile these two

14:04.320 --> 14:10.380
raving models of physics, of general relativity and quantum mechanics, which, you know, no

14:10.380 --> 14:12.580
one has figured out how to sort of bring those two together.

14:13.240 --> 14:13.680
Exactly.

14:13.680 --> 14:16.740
And I think that there is a way of putting the two together.

14:16.860 --> 14:21.000
That's what basically I spent my life on.

14:21.140 --> 14:22.980
I've been paid all my life for trying.

14:22.980 --> 14:30.820
And with friends and colleagues, we have a tentative way of bringing together quantum mechanics and Einstein

14:30.820 --> 14:31.200
theories.

14:32.080 --> 14:33.720
General relativity, I hope it works.

14:33.820 --> 14:34.500
I hope it's good.

14:34.620 --> 14:40.240
And I think it is understanding something more about the world, something deeper about

14:40.240 --> 14:40.720
the world.

14:40.860 --> 14:44.140
But look, if you have a girlfriend, you understand her better.

14:44.360 --> 14:46.120
Do you really think you know everything about her?

14:46.120 --> 14:47.400
No, of course not.

14:47.580 --> 14:48.560
And so it's reality.

14:48.920 --> 14:49.500
It's exactly.

14:49.640 --> 14:50.300
It's like a girlfriend.

14:50.440 --> 14:54.820
And it's wonderful when you open your eyes, when you communicate with a person.

14:55.180 --> 14:57.860
And it's equally wonderful when you communicate with nature.

14:57.980 --> 15:00.100
You see something about nature you didn't see before.

15:01.040 --> 15:03.860
So, generativity is this kind of discovery.

15:04.180 --> 15:10.120
Wow, we realize that this idea of a rigid space in which we are in a common time, this

15:10.120 --> 15:11.900
is all approximation.

15:12.240 --> 15:13.240
It's not the reality, right?

15:13.280 --> 15:14.720
It's far more complicated than that.

15:14.720 --> 15:17.560
So, you see beyond it, and you see more.

15:18.640 --> 15:23.300
But I don't have the ambition that this, I've got the looking to the, talking to God

15:23.300 --> 15:24.640
and getting to the bottom.

15:24.640 --> 15:28.540
I mean, no, you know, not finding ultimate reality, but maybe getting a little closer

15:28.540 --> 15:31.540
to what is real.

15:32.020 --> 15:35.620
A little better understanding of this abyssal thing.

15:36.020 --> 15:38.500
We don't know the fundamental law of quantum gravity yet.

15:38.580 --> 15:39.960
We don't have a unified theory.

15:40.180 --> 15:41.720
So, there's so many things we don't know.

15:41.720 --> 15:51.900
We live in an open, marvelous, beautiful, mysterious, sacred universe of which we understand a lot.

15:51.900 --> 15:54.260
And we are happy to understand a lot.

15:54.340 --> 15:57.620
It is an incredibly beautiful feeling to understand more.

15:57.620 --> 16:01.540
And I want to contribute to understand more.

16:01.540 --> 16:07.800
So, we're at a scientific conference right now in Italy, 30 miles south of Siena.

16:08.140 --> 16:09.080
Beautiful countryside.

16:09.080 --> 16:09.900
Beautiful countryside.

16:10.060 --> 16:12.340
We're part of this Island of Knowledge think tank.

16:12.640 --> 16:16.820
And the point of this particular meeting that we're at is asking some really big questions.

16:16.960 --> 16:18.640
One is, is there ultimate reality?

16:18.640 --> 16:21.400
I mean, that is one of the questions that's been put on the table here.

16:21.580 --> 16:25.380
And I have to say, you had a fascinating answer to that.

16:25.480 --> 16:30.160
I mean, you said when you were young, this was an agonizing question for you.

16:30.380 --> 16:31.180
But no longer.

16:31.300 --> 16:34.220
You say, you say it's a bad question and we should not ask that question.

16:34.340 --> 16:37.120
So, before we get to why you say this is a bad question,

16:37.500 --> 16:41.740
I want to take you back to when you were young and when you agonized over that question.

16:43.740 --> 16:44.100
Okay.

16:44.540 --> 16:47.660
While Carlo thinks about this, we're going to take a short break.

16:48.640 --> 16:49.640
We'll be right back.

